Instead of building possible prefixes, this keeps an end index and moves it left while the current last digit is even. When an odd digit is reached, the prefix from `0` through that index is ready.

A string such as `"2468"` has no valid ending, so the method returns `""`. The input string is not changed.

```java
class Solution {    
    public String largeOddNum(String s) {
        int end = s.length() - 1;

        while (end >= 0) {
            int digit = s.charAt(end) - '0';
            if (digit % 2 == 1) {
                return s.substring(0, end + 1);
            }
            end--;
        }

        return "";
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` in the worst case.

Space complexity: `O(1)` besides the result string.

The odd digits are only `1`, `3`, `5`, `7`, and `9`, so a set makes the check easy to read. The loop starts at the end because the first odd digit found there gives the longest valid prefix.

Dry run with `"52"`:

- `2` is not in the set, so move left.
- `5` is in the set, so return `"5"`.

This also handles an input whose final digit is already odd by returning the whole string immediately.

```python
class Solution:  
    def largeOddNum(self, s: str) -> str:
        odd_digits = {'1', '3', '5', '7', '9'}

        for i in range(len(s) - 1, -1, -1):
            if s[i] in odd_digits:
                return s[:i + 1]

        return ""
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.

Space complexity: `O(1)` for the fixed-size digit set, excluding the returned slice.

The last digit decides whether a number is odd. So the useful position is the rightmost odd digit. Once it is found, the answer is the prefix ending there.

For `s = "35420"`, the scan checks `0`, `2`, and `4` before reaching `5`. The returned prefix is `"35"`. If every digit is even, the result is an empty string.

