class Solution{	
public:		
    string largeOddNum(string& s){
        for (int i = static_cast<int>(s.size()) - 1; i >= 0; --i) {
            int digit = s[i] - '0';
            if (digit % 2 == 1) {
                return s.substr(0, i + 1);
            }
        }
        return "";
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.

Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space, not counting the returned string.

A prefix is valid when its final character is odd. The loop searches backward until it finds that final character, then returns everything before it as well.

For `"12040"`, every digit is even, so the answer is empty. For `"00120"`, the found prefix is `"001"`; the small cleanup in this template removes leading zeroes from that returned value.

```go
func largeOddNum(s string) string {
    n := len(s)
    for i := n - 1; i >= 0; i-- {
        digit := s[i] - '0'
        if digit%2 == 1 {
            res := s[:i+1]

            firstNonZero := 0
            for firstNonZero < len(res)-1 && res[firstNonZero] == '0' {
                firstNonZero++
            }
            return res[firstNonZero:]
        }
    }
    return ""
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`, including the possible leading-zero cleanup.

Space complexity: `O(n)` for the returned string slice in the result representation.

This version stores the index of the last odd digit. Scanning from right to left means the first odd digit found is already the furthest possible ending point. Only after the scan do we create the answer substring.

Dry run on `"420635"`: the last character is `5`, so the saved boundary is the final index and the whole string is returned. If the string is empty, the method also returns an empty string without indexing into it.

```csharp
class Solution
{
    public string LargeOddNum(string s)
    {
        int end = -1;

        for (int i = s.Length - 1; i >= 0; i--)
        {
            int digit = s[i] - '0';
            if (digit % 2 == 1)
            {
                end = i;
                break;
            }
        }

        if (end == -1)
        {
            return "";
        }

        return s.Substring(0, end + 1);
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` for the scan, with the substring copy taking up to `O(n)` as well.

Space complexity: `O(n)` for the returned substring.

## Approach

1. Traverse from the end of the string to find the **last odd digit**.
2. If no odd digit exists, return an empty string.
3. Traverse from the beginning to find the **first non-zero digit**.
4. Return the substring from the first non-zero digit up to the last odd digit.

## Solution
```cpp[]
class Solution{	
public:		
    string largeOddNum(string& s){

        // back traversal...finding first odd number(technically last)
        int last_odd_index = -1;
        for(int i = s.size() - 1; i>=0; i--){
            if(s[i]%2==1){
                last_odd_index = i;
                break;
            }
        }

    if (last_odd_index == -1) {
            return "";
        }

        // front traversal for finding first non-zero char
        int first_non_zero_index = 0;
        for(int i = 0; i<s.size(); i++){
            if(s[i]!='0'){
                first_non_zero_index = i;
                break;
            }
        }

        // length of substring
        int sub_length = last_odd_index - first_non_zero_index + 1;
        return s.substr(first_non_zero_index, sub_length);


    }
};
```

## Complexity
**Time Complexity:** O(n)
**Space Complexity:** O(1)

